      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-259318",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/b3/b3fed3309793174564ac5f12062b17548b5293faf65e0dbfb8b654b200d459b2.png)\n\nConsider the weighted graph above. Dijkstra's algorithm for finding the shortest paths is applied to the graph, starting at $v_{1}$. After considering all the vertices connected to $v_{1}$, we have the following configuration, where the priority queue is ordered left to right from shortest to longest path found:\n\n| Priority Queue: | $v_{3}$, $v_{4}$, $v_{2}$, $v_{5}$, $v_{6}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ | $v_{6}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Distances | $0$ | $9$ | $6$ | $8$ | $\u221e$ | $\u221e$ |\n| Previous | $-1$ | $v_{1}$ | $v_{1}$ | $v_{1}$ |  |  |\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nOn the next step of the algorithm, we pop the vertex\n{{field-1}}\nfrom the head of the priority queue and visit its neighbors\n{{field-2}}.\n\nWe set the distance to\n{{field-3}}\nequal to\n{{field-4}},\nand set the previous vertex\n{{field-5}}.\n\nAfter sorting, the priority queue is\n{{field-6}}."
